## God Class Anti-Pattern

## What It Looks Like

```typescript
// ❌ The God class — does EVERYTHING
class ApplicationManager {
  // User management
  createUser(email: string, password: string) {}
  updateUser(id: string, data: Partial<User>) {}
  deleteUser(id: string) {}
  sendPasswordResetEmail(email: string) {}

  // Order management
  createOrder(userId: string, items: Item[]) {}
  cancelOrder(orderId: string) {}
  processPayment(orderId: string, card: CardDetails) {}
  generateInvoice(orderId: string): PDF {}

  // Product management
  addProduct(data: ProductData) {}
  updateStock(productId: string, qty: number) {}
  applyDiscount(productId: string, pct: number) {}

  // Email
  sendWelcomeEmail(user: User) {}
  sendOrderConfirmation(order: Order) {}
  sendShippingUpdate(order: Order) {}

  // Reports
  generateSalesReport(from: Date, to: Date) {}
  exportToCsv(data: unknown[]) {}

  // ... 200 more methods
}
```

## Why It's Harmful

```
❌ Untestable: to test generateInvoice() you load the whole monster class
❌ Merge conflicts: every developer touches this file constantly
❌ Fragile: change createUser() and generateSalesReport() breaks
❌ Incomprehensible: no one understands the whole class
❌ Can't reuse: can't use invoice generation without dragging in user management
```

## Refactored

```typescript
// ✅ Each class has one clear responsibility
class UserService {
  createUser(email: string, password: string) {}
  updateUser(id: string, data: Partial<User>) {}
}

class OrderService {
  createOrder(userId: string, items: Item[]) {}
  cancelOrder(orderId: string) {}
}

class PaymentService {
  processPayment(orderId: string, card: CardDetails) {}
}

class EmailService {
  sendWelcomeEmail(user: User) {}
  sendOrderConfirmation(order: Order) {}
}

class ReportingService {
  generateSalesReport(from: Date, to: Date) {}
}
```

