## YAGNI vs KISS

## YAGNI — You Aren't Gonna Need It

```
Origin: Extreme Programming (Kent Beck, Ron Jeffries)
Meaning: don't add functionality until it's actually needed

Scope → WHAT to build
```

```typescript
// ❌ YAGNI violation: building a "plugin system" nobody asked for
class NotificationService {
  private plugins: NotificationPlugin[] = []; // for future extensibility
  registerPlugin(plugin: NotificationPlugin) { this.plugins.push(plugin); }

  send(msg: string) {
    this.plugins.forEach(p => p.beforeSend?.(msg)); // nobody uses this yet
    console.log(msg);
    this.plugins.forEach(p => p.afterSend?.(msg));
  }
}

// ✅ YAGNI: build only what's needed now
function sendNotification(msg: string) {
  console.log(msg);
}
// When you need plugins, add them. Don't pre-build complexity.
```

## KISS — Keep It Simple, Stupid

```
Meaning: the code you DO write should be as simple as possible

Complexity → HOW to build it
```

```typescript
// ❌ KISS violation: unnecessary complexity
const getFullName = (user: User) =>
  [user.firstName, user.middleName, user.lastName]
    .filter(Boolean)
    .join(' ') || user.email.split('@')[0];

// ✅ KISS: simpler, and handles 99% of cases
const getFullName = (user: User) =>
  `${user.firstName} ${user.lastName}`.trim();
```

## Together

```
YAGNI → Don't build the plugin system until someone needs plugins
KISS  → When you do build it, make it as simple as possible

Both guard against over-engineering at different levels:
  YAGNI: feature/abstraction scope
  KISS:  implementation complexity
```

## The Tension

```
YAGNI says "don't abstract yet".
DRY says "don't repeat yourself".
Sometimes they conflict: the first time you repeat code, YAGNI says wait;
  the third time, extract the abstraction.
The Rule of Three: wait until you see repetition 3× before abstracting.
```

