## Testing Private Methods and Fields

## The Principle: Test via the Public Interface

```typescript
// ❌ You want to test this private method:
class OrderService {
  private calculateTax(amount: number, country: string): number {
    if (country === 'GB') return amount * 0.2;
    if (country === 'US') return amount * 0.1;
    return 0;
  }

  placeOrder(items: Item[], country: string): OrderSummary {
    const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0);
    const tax = this.calculateTax(subtotal, country); // private
    return { subtotal, tax, total: subtotal + tax };
  }
}

// ✅ Test the observable behaviour via placeOrder() — tests calculateTax() implicitly
test('applies 20% tax for UK orders', () => {
  const service = new OrderService();
  const summary = service.placeOrder([{ price: 100 }], 'GB');
  expect(summary.tax).toBe(20);
  expect(summary.total).toBe(120);
});
```

## When Testing Private Methods Is a Design Smell

```typescript
// The private method is too complex to test indirectly?
// Extract it into its own class!

// ✅ Extract to a testable class
class TaxCalculator {
  calculate(amount: number, country: string): number { // now public and testable
    if (country === 'GB') return amount * 0.2;
    if (country === 'US') return amount * 0.1;
    return 0;
  }
}

// Now test TaxCalculator directly:
test('GB tax rate is 20%', () => {
  const calc = new TaxCalculator();
  expect(calc.calculate(100, 'GB')).toBe(20);
});

// OrderService uses it via injection
class OrderService {
  constructor(private taxCalc: TaxCalculator = new TaxCalculator()) {}
  // ...
}
```

## When You Absolutely Must Access Private Members

```typescript
// TypeScript: cast to any (use sparingly — it's a smell)
const result = (service as any).calculateTax(100, 'GB');

// Or use @ts-ignore for one line
// @ts-ignore
const result = service.calculateTax(100, 'GB');

// These break if you refactor the class — tightly coupled to implementation
```

