## Cohesion vs Coupling

## Cohesion: Within a Module

```
High cohesion → module has ONE focused responsibility
Low cohesion  → module has MANY unrelated responsibilities

Goal: HIGH cohesion
```

```typescript
// ❌ Low cohesion: UserService does too many unrelated things
class UserService {
  createUser(data: UserData) {}
  generatePDF(order: Order) {}         // what does PDF have to do with users?
  sendMarketingEmail(campaign: Email) {} // unrelated
  calculateTax(amount: number) {}       // unrelated
}

// ✅ High cohesion: each class has one clear purpose
class UserService { createUser(data: UserData) {} }
class PdfGenerator { generatePDF(order: Order) {} }
class MarketingService { sendCampaign(email: Email) {} }
class TaxCalculator { calculate(amount: number) {} }
```

## Coupling: Between Modules

```
High coupling → change in A forces changes in B
Low coupling  → A and B interact through stable interfaces

Goal: LOW coupling
```

```typescript
// ❌ High coupling: OrderService directly instantiates PostgresDB
class OrderService {
  private db = new PostgresDB({ host: 'localhost', port: 5432 }); // tight!
  // Change DB config or switch DB → must edit OrderService
}

// ✅ Low coupling: depends on interface
interface Database { query<T>(sql: string): Promise<T[]>; }
class OrderService {
  constructor(private db: Database) {} // interface decouples from implementation
}
```

## The Relationship

```
High cohesion → smaller, focused classes → fewer reasons to depend on others → lower coupling
Low cohesion → fat classes → many things depending on them → higher coupling

"High cohesion and low coupling" is the mantra of good OO design.
```

## Types of Coupling (Low to High)

```
Data coupling    → share only simple data (function parameters)  ← best
Stamp coupling   → share complex data structures
Control coupling → one module tells another what to do (flag parameters)
Content coupling → one module reads/writes internals of another  ← worst
```

